@page "/features/content-security-policy"

<DocExamples Title="Content Security Policy">

    <Description>
        <p>
            A Content Security Policy (CSP) tells the browser which styles and scripts a page may run. This page lists
            what Blazor-ApexCharts needs from a strict policy, one that does not allow <code>'unsafe-inline'</code> styles.
        </p>

        <h4>Styles: set <code>Chart.Nonce</code></h4>
        <p>
            ApexCharts adds its stylesheet to the page as a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> element, and each legend adds one
            too. A <code>style-src</code> without <code>'unsafe-inline'</code> blocks both, and the chart renders
            unstyled. Allow them with a nonce: put <code>'nonce-&lt;value&gt;'</code> in <code>style-src</code> and the
            same value in <code>Chart.Nonce</code>. ApexCharts copies it onto every <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> element it adds.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                The stylesheet is shared by every chart on the page and is added by the first chart that renders, so
                set the nonce on every chart, or once in the global options
                (<code>AddApexCharts(o =&gt; o.GlobalOptions = ...)</code> or <code>IApexChartService.SetGlobalOptionsAsync</code>).
            </li>
            <li>
                A nonce protects only while it is unpredictable, so generate a fresh one for each request on the server,
                send it in the CSP header, and pass the same value to your charts, for example from <code>App.razor</code>,
                where the <code>HttpContext</code> is available. A Blazor app keeps the policy of the page it loaded with,
                so one nonce per page load is enough.
            </li>
            <li>
                A page served as a static file cannot vary a nonce per request. There, a hash source works instead:
                the browser console names the <code>'sha256-...'</code> value of each blocked <code>&lt;style&gt;</code>
                element. The hash changes when the stylesheet does, so update it when you upgrade the package.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Inline style attributes</h4>
        <p>
            The Blazor components add no <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> elements and no <code>style</code> attributes of their
            own. A few tooltips that ApexCharts builds as HTML (the range bar tooltip and bar goal markers) color their
            markers with a <code>style</code> attribute. A strict policy blocks those, and the markers lose their
            color. If that matters, allow attributes alone with <code>style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'</code>, which
            leaves <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> elements under the nonce. Styles in your own Razor tooltip templates follow
            the same rules as the rest of your markup.
        </p>

        <h4>Scripts: JavaScript functions need <code>'unsafe-eval'</code></h4>
        <p>
            Options that take a JavaScript function as a string (formatters, <code>CustomFunction</code> and similar) are
            turned into functions in the browser with <code>eval</code>, which <code>script-src</code> allows only with
            <code>'unsafe-eval'</code>. Without it, rendering a chart that uses one fails with an <code>EvalError</code>.
            The component also writes such a function for you when you use:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>the <code>FormatXAxisLabel</code> or <code>FormatYAxisLabel</code> parameters,</li>
            <li>a toolbar custom icon with <code>OnClick</code>,</li>
            <li><code>OnClick</code>, <code>OnMouseEnter</code> or <code>OnMouseLeave</code> on an annotation or its label.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Charts that use none of these, including the chart events (<code>OnDataPointSelection</code> and the like)
            and Razor tooltips (<code>ApexPointTooltip</code>), need no <code>'unsafe-eval'</code>.
            Blazor WebAssembly itself needs <code>'wasm-unsafe-eval'</code>, which is a separate keyword.
        </p>
    </Description>

    <ChildContent>
        <CodeSnippet Title="Chart nonce" ClassName=@typeof(NonceChart).ToString()>
            <Description>
                <Alert>
                    With a header such as <code>Content-Security-Policy: style-src 'self' 'nonce-r4nd0m'</code>, set
                    <code>Chart.Nonce = "r4nd0m"</code>. Use a new random value for each request in a real app.
                </Alert>
            </Description>
            <Snippet>
                <NonceChart />
            </Snippet>
        </CodeSnippet>
    </ChildContent>

</DocExamples>
